        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much does a treadmill weigh?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It varies widely by type. As typical ranges, walking pads weigh roughly 50 to 75 lb, folding home treadmills roughly 150 to 250 lb, and non-folding or light-commercial machines roughly 250 to 400 lb or more. Most of the weight sits at the front where the motor is. Check your manual or the manufacturer's site for your exact model.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How many people does it take to move a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              One person can roll a treadmill across a level floor on its transport wheels and can usually carry a walking pad. Any full-size machine on stairs needs at least two people, and three is safer for anything heavier than about 250 lb — two handling the load and one spotting the route.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you move a treadmill upstairs by yourself?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not a full-size one, and you should not try. A treadmill's weight is concentrated at the motor end, which makes it unstable on a stair, and the person below the load takes most of the force with the least control. A walking pad is the realistic exception for a solo move.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should a treadmill be moved lying down or standing up?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Follow your manual if it gives guidance. As a general rule, a non-folding treadmill travels best flat on its feet, ideally with the console and uprights removed. A folding machine can usually travel upright if the deck is locked, strapped and the machine is lashed so it cannot tip. Avoid laying any treadmill on its side or upside down.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need to take a treadmill apart to move it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not usually for a folding machine moving within a house. Removing the console and uprights is worth doing for a non-folding treadmill, or whenever the measured route is too narrow for the assembled machine. Label every bolt and part, and unplug the console cable at its connector rather than pulling the wire.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What should I check after moving a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Let it reach room temperature before powering up if it has been in the cold, then level it, tighten every fastener, and run it slowly with nobody on it to check the belt stays centred. Check belt tension when you first step on, confirm the safety key stop works, and walk before you run.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

